Prometheus is running on the monitoring VM at:
http://localhost:9090 (public: http://35.187.253.23:9090)

Grafana is running on the same VM at:
http://35.187.253.23:3000

Your Node.js app metrics are at:
http://10.148.0.9:8080/metrics (already in prometheus.yml as job_name: app-metrics)

1. Install & start Grafana (one-time)

On monitoring-vm:

cd ~
wget https://dl.grafana.com/oss/release/grafana_11.1.0_amd64.deb
sudo dpkg -i grafana_11.1.0_amd64.deb

# Enable & start Grafana service
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
sudo systemctl start grafana-server
sudo systemctl status grafana-server


Open in browser:

http://<MONITORING_PUBLIC_IP>:3000
e.g. http://35.187.253.23:3000

First login:

Username: admin

Password: admin (you’ll be prompted to change it)

2. Add Prometheus as a data source in Grafana

In Grafana left menu, click ⚙️ Settings → Data sources.

Click “Add data source”.

Choose Prometheus.

In HTTP → URL, set:

http://localhost:9090


(Grafana and Prometheus are on the same VM.)

Leave the rest as defaults.

Click “Save & test”.
You should see “Data source is working”.

Now Grafana can query all metrics that Prometheus is scraping (Prometheus itself + app metrics).

3. Verify metrics in Grafana (Explore)

Left menu → Explore.

At the top, choose Data source: prometheus (the one you just added).

In the query box, type:

app_request_count


Time range: Last 1 hour.

Click “Run query”.

You should see the time-series line like in your screenshot:

__name__="app_request_count", instance="10.148.0.9:8080", job="app-metrics"

If you don’t see it, hit your app a few times:

for i in {1..20}; do curl http://10.148.0.9:8080; sleep 1; done


Then re-run the query.

4. Import the Node.js Application Dashboard

This is the one showing CPU, memory, event loop lag, handlers, etc.

In Grafana left menu, click Dashboards → New → Import.

You have two options:

Using a Grafana.com ID (for example, a Node.js dashboard like 11159), or

Using JSON (if you saved the JSON we used).

In the Import screen:

Paste the dashboard ID or JSON.

Click Load.

Under “Prometheus data source”, select your prometheus data source.

Click Import.

The dashboard will now show:

Process CPU Usage

Event Loop Lag

Process Memory Usage

Active Handlers / Requests

etc., all for instance 10.148.0.9:8080.

5. Import the “Prometheus 2.0 Overview” Dashboard

This is the one that was previously N/A. It works only when Prometheus itself is a scrape target.

You already fixed prometheus.yml with:

- job_name: 'prometheus'
  static_configs:
    - targets: ['localhost:9090']


Now import the dashboard:

Dashboards → New → Import.

Use the Prometheus 2.0 overview dashboard from Grafana.com (e.g. ID 3662) or JSON.

Click Load.

Map Prometheus data source → prometheus.
